import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

public class SquarePermutations {
    public static int squarePermutations(int[] A) {
        Set<Integer> seen = new HashSet<>();
        int count = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < A.length; i++) {
            for (int j = i + 1; j < A.length; j++) {
                int sum = A[i] + A[j];
                if (isSquare(sum)) {
                    if (!seen.contains(sum)) {
                        count++;
                        seen.add(sum);
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return count;
    }

    private static boolean isSquare(int n) {
        int sqrt = (int) Math.sqrt(n);
        return sqrt * sqrt == n;
    }
}
```
In the above solution, we use a set to keep track of the unique sums of adjacent elements in the input array. If the sum is a perfect square, we add it to the set and increment a count. In the end, we return the count of unique perfect square sums.

The `isSquare` method checks if a given integer is a perfect square by finding its square root using the `Math.sqrt` method and checking if it is equal to the integer.

The time complexity of the solution is O(n^2), where n is the length of the input array, since we have a nested loop that iterates over each pair of adjacent elements in the array. The space complexity is O(n), since we create a set to keep track of the unique sums.

